{"nodes": [{"id": "docs_writing_a_transport_package_model", "label": "A transport is an ordinary npm package", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/writing-a-transport.md", "source_location": "lines 3, 129-141", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "No fork, no pull request, nothing merged upstream: publish it, npm install it, name it in a config file. Convention is <scope>/deaddrop-transport-<name>, and local paths work for development."}, {"id": "docs_writing_a_transport_define_transport", "label": "defineTransport", "file_type": "code", "source_file": "docs/writing-a-transport.md", "source_location": "lines 10-39", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Entry point from @fyrlabs/dead-drop-transport-sdk taking id, capabilities, parseConfig and create."}, {"id": "docs_writing_a_transport_capabilities", "label": "Declared capabilities", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/writing-a-transport.md", "source_location": "lines 20-28, 121", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "kind, ordering, binaryPayloads, delete, watch, orderedList, maxPayloadBytes. The conformance suite adapts to what you declare, and declaring a capability you do not have will fail."}, {"id": "docs_writing_a_transport_parse_config", "label": "parseConfig", "file_type": "code", "source_file": "docs/writing-a-transport.md", "source_location": "lines 30-34, 146", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Throws DeadDropError('CONFIG_INVALID') at start-up so a typo fails immediately rather than at 3am, with a message that says how to fix it."}, {"id": "docs_writing_a_transport_store_contract", "label": "StoreTransport interface", "file_type": "code", "source_file": "docs/writing-a-transport.md", "source_location": "lines 46-57", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "put, get, list, delete, optional watch, plus health and close. Four required methods; dead-drop supplies framing, encryption, chunking, acknowledgement, retries with jitter, deduplication, dead-lettering, health-based routing, failover, metrics and tracing."}, {"id": "docs_writing_a_transport_get_absence", "label": "Rule: get on a missing key resolves undefined", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/writing-a-transport.md", "source_location": "line 61", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Absence is normal: another consumer may have deleted the object between the list and the get, so it must never throw for absence."}, {"id": "docs_writing_a_transport_delete_idempotent", "label": "Rule: delete is idempotent", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/writing-a-transport.md", "source_location": "line 62", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Deleting a key that does not exist must succeed, because concurrent consumers race on the same key."}, {"id": "docs_writing_a_transport_put_durable", "label": "Rule: put is durable when it resolves", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/writing-a-transport.md", "source_location": "line 63", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "If the backend batches, resolve after the batch lands, not when it is queued, or at-least-once delivery is silently broken."}, {"id": "docs_writing_a_transport_list_path_boundaries", "label": "Rule: list matches on path boundaries", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/writing-a-transport.md", "source_location": "line 64", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "inbox/peer-a must not return inbox/peer-abc/..., or one peer reads another's inbox by prefix accident."}, {"id": "docs_writing_a_transport_put_if_absent", "label": "Rule: put with ifAbsent is atomic create-or-fail", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/writing-a-transport.md", "source_location": "line 65", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Use the backend's create-or-fail primitive; a check-then-write race defeats the purpose."}, {"id": "docs_writing_a_transport_assert_valid_key", "label": "assertValidKey / assertValidPrefix", "file_type": "code", "source_file": "docs/writing-a-transport.md", "source_location": "line 66", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Call before interpolating a key into a path or URL; the conformance suite checks that you do."}, {"id": "docs_writing_a_transport_concurrency", "label": "Rule: concurrency is expected", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/writing-a-transport.md", "source_location": "line 67", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Two calls can be in flight at once."}, {"id": "docs_writing_a_transport_start_after", "label": "startAfter in ListOptions", "file_type": "code", "source_file": "docs/writing-a-transport.md", "source_location": "line 69", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Subscribers use it to ask what is new since a key, which on a remote backend is the difference between one cheap call and one that grows with retention."}, {"id": "docs_writing_a_transport_health", "label": "health() and TransportHealth", "file_type": "code", "source_file": "docs/writing-a-transport.md", "source_location": "lines 71-87", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "The transport manager scores on health, recent reliability, latency and rate-limit headroom, so an honest degraded is what makes failover work. Report degraded for working-but-prefer-something-else: high latency, a nearly exhausted rate limit."}, {"id": "docs_writing_a_transport_real_probe", "label": "Probe something that can genuinely fail", "file_type": "rationale", "source_file": "docs/writing-a-transport.md", "source_location": "lines 76, 87, 151", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "A stat on a disconnected network mount often succeeds; a write does not. A no-op probe reports health that is not there."}, {"id": "docs_writing_a_transport_error_codes", "label": "DeadDropError code table", "file_type": "code", "source_file": "docs/writing-a-transport.md", "source_location": "lines 89-99", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "The code decides what happens next. TRANSPORT_ERROR and TIMEOUT retry then fail over; RATE_LIMITED retries and honours retryAfterMs exactly instead of guessing; UNAUTHORIZED, BAD_REQUEST, PAYLOAD_TOO_LARGE and UNSUPPORTED are not failed over because they fail the same way on every backend; CANCELLED is neither retried nor counted against the circuit breaker."}, {"id": "docs_writing_a_transport_conformance_suite", "label": "registerConformanceTests", "file_type": "code", "source_file": "docs/writing-a-transport.md", "source_location": "lines 101-121", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Framework-agnostic: returns plain { name, run } cases so it works with vitest, node:test, jest or a bare script and pulls no test runner into the dependency tree. settleMs is non-zero only for eventually consistent backends."}, {"id": "docs_writing_a_transport_native_transport", "label": "Native transports (kind: 'native')", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/writing-a-transport.md", "source_location": "lines 123-127", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "For backends with their own delivery semantics (AMQP, MQTT, a websocket relay): implement send/subscribe and dead-drop stays out of the way rather than synthesising acknowledgements. Most people should not want this; if the backend is storage, store is less work and more correct."}, {"id": "docs_writing_a_transport_publish_checklist", "label": "Pre-publish checklist", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/writing-a-transport.md", "source_location": "lines 143-152", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Conformance passes, parseConfig rejects loudly, credentials come from the environment never committed config, nothing secret reaches a log or error message, close() is idempotent and releases everything, maxPayloadBytes is real so chunking is sized right, health() probes something that can fail."}, {"id": "docs_writing_a_transport_credential_hygiene", "label": "Third-party credential and log hygiene", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/writing-a-transport.md", "source_location": "lines 147-148", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "Credentials from the environment or a credential helper only; strip inline credentials from urls before anything is logged. Mirrors the core runtime's own rules onto packages the project does not control."}, {"id": "docs_writing_a_transport_max_payload_bytes", "label": "maxPayloadBytes and chunk sizing", "file_type": "concept", "source_file": "docs/writing-a-transport.md", "source_location": "lines 27, 42, 150", "source_url": null, "captured_at": null, "author": null, "contributor": null, "rationale": "dead-drop chunks to this number, so an inaccurate value produces frames the backend rejects."}], "edges": [{"source": "docs_security_model_path_traversal_defense", "target": "docs_writing_a_transport_assert_valid_key", "relation": "semantically_similar_to", "confidence": "INFERRED", "confidence_score": 0.85, "source_file": "docs/writing-a-transport.md", "source_location": "security-model.md line 72, writing-a-transport.md line 66", "weight": 1.0}, {"source": "docs_security_model_url_credential_stripping", "target": "docs_writing_a_transport_credential_hygiene", "relation": "semantically_similar_to", "confidence": "INFERRED", "confidence_score": 0.95, "source_file": "docs/writing-a-transport.md", "source_location": "lines 147-148", "weight": 1.0}, {"source": "docs_security_model_log_redaction", "target": "docs_writing_a_transport_credential_hygiene", "relation": "conceptually_related_to", "confidence": "INFERRED", "confidence_score": 0.85, "source_file": "docs/writing-a-transport.md", "source_location": "line 148", "weight": 1.0}, {"source": "docs_security_model_dos_limits", "target": "docs_writing_a_transport_max_payload_bytes", "relation": "conceptually_related_to", "confidence": "INFERRED", "confidence_score": 0.75, "source_file": "docs/writing-a-transport.md", "source_location": "lines 27, 150", "weight": 1.0}, {"source": "docs_security_model_gzip_bomb", "target": "docs_writing_a_transport_error_codes", "relation": "shares_data_with", "confidence": "INFERRED", "confidence_score": 0.95, "source_file": "docs/writing-a-transport.md", "source_location": "line 98", "weight": 1.0}, {"source": "docs_writing_a_transport_define_transport", "target": "docs_writing_a_transport_capabilities", "relation": "references", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1.0, "source_file": "docs/writing-a-transport.md", "source_location": "lines 20-28", "weight": 1.0}, {"source": "docs_writing_a_transport_define_transport", "target": "docs_writing_a_transport_parse_config", "relation": "references", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1.0, "source_file": "docs/writing-a-transport.md", "source_location": "lines 30-34", "weight": 1.0}, {"source": "docs_writing_a_transport_define_transport", "target": "docs_writing_a_transport_store_contract", "relation": "implements", "confidence": "EXTRACTED", "confidence_score": 1.0, "source_file": "docs/writing-a-transport.md", "source_location": "lines 36-57", "weig